   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises to adopt new business models, technology and techniques to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Department of Labour &amp; Employment Services in Vanuatu has undertaken the design of the very first one-stop virtual portal where business development service providers, existing and potential entrepreneurs and job seekers can access information on self-employment opportunities. This was done in partnership with the Vanuatu Chamber of Commerce and Industry and the Vanuatu Council of Trades Union, the website aims at making fully accessible the information on the Vanuatu&#x2019; job market. The objective of the website is to facilitate digital employment services in order to reduce the barriers to access to the labour market information. The services provided, including advertisement of job vacancies, registration for job seekers profile, job-candidate matching and face-to-face support are free of charge. https://employmentvanuatu.gov.vu/n

For the first time in Vanuatu&#x2019;s history, a member payment system via a mobile app (M-Vatu) to support access to social security was launched by the Vanuatu Minister of Finance and Economic Management in June 2021. Close to 10,000 informal sector micro entrepreneurs and workers now have access to social security through the launch of a mobile app payment system with was done in partnership in the Vanuatu National Provident Fund, Vodafone, International Labour Organisation and the United Nations Capital Development Fund. This system allows informal sector workers make direct contributions to the Vanuatu National Provident Fund who can now make their contributions directly into their accounts using M-Vatu, a new and innovative platform.
https://www.ilo.org/suva/public-information/WCMS_823454/lang--en/index.htm
 ILO contributed to the training for business development service providers, provided access to  ILO&#x2019;s activity based learning tools particularly C-BED , undertook review of current training programmes of business development service providers, including the feasible options for on-line delivery, creation of web portal on employment services including self-employment, development of business awareness materials for seasonal workers and the - digitization of materials to support delivery by business development providers. 
https://www.ilo.org/suva/public-information/WCMS_823439/lang--en/index.htm</narrative>
